## Analyzing Student Performance

A `Student` struct holds grades across multiple subjects. Methods can compute averages and find the strongest subject.

### The Student Struct

```xiom
type Student = {
  name: Str;
  math: Int;
  science: Int;
  english: Int;
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let student = Student{ name: "Vivian", math: 92, science: 88, english: 95 };
  io.println(student.name);
  io.println(student.math);
  return 0;
}
```

### Computing the Average

```xiom
fn Student.average(self) -> Float64 {
  let total = math + science + english;
  return total as Float64 / 3.0;
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let s = Student{ name: "Vivian", math: 92, science: 88, english: 95 };
  let avg = s.average();
  io.println(avg);  // 91.666666...
  return 0;
}
```

Note: `total as Float64` converts the Int sum to Float64 so division produces a precise decimal.

### Finding the Highest Grade

```xiom
fn Student.highest_subject(self) -> Str {
  if math >= science && math >= english {
    return "Math";
  }
  if science >= math && science >= english {
    return "Science";
  }
  return "English";
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let s = Student{ name: "Wyatt", math: 78, science: 85, english: 82 };
  let best = s.highest_subject();
  io.println(best);  // Science
  return 0;
}
```

### Letter Grade Method

```xiom
fn Student.letter_grade(self) -> Str {
  let avg = average();
  if avg >= 90.0 {
    return "A";
  }
  if avg >= 80.0 {
    return "B";
  }
  if avg >= 70.0 {
    return "C";
  }
  return "F";
}
```

Notice how `letter_grade` calls `average()` — methods can call other methods on the same struct!

